  "worked_solution": "First, we decompose all of the terms into their various factors. The $16$ in the numerator becomes $2\\cdot 2\\cdot 2\\cdot 2,$ and the $20(y-3)$ in the denominator becomes $2\\cdot 2\\cdot 5\\cdot (y-3).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{16}{20(y-3)}=\\frac{2\\cdot 2\\cdot 2\\cdot 2}{2\\cdot 2\\cdot 5\\cdot (y-3)}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we cancel out all the pairs of common factors in the numerator and denominator. In this case, there are two pairs of $2$'s.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2\\cdot 2\\cdot 2\\cdot 2}{2\\cdot 2\\cdot 5\\cdot (y-3)} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{2}\\cdot \\cancel{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot 2}{\\cancel{2}\\cdot \\cancel{2}\\cdot 5\\cdot (y-3)} & = \\\\ \\frac{2\\cdot 2}{5\\cdot (y-3)} & = \\\\ \\frac{4}{5(y-3)}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the expression simplifies to $\\frac{4}{5(y-3)}.$",
